Yes.

If you pretend you’re telling the story to God, you can’t rely on information to make it interesting. He already knows what happened.

So what remains is:

what you noticed, what you loved, what you feared, what you misunderstood, what changed you, and why this moment deserved to be remembered at all.

That changes storytelling from reporting events into bearing witness.

You also stop performing for the crowd. You don’t have to sound clever, prove you were right, or manipulate the listener into feeling something. Your audience already knows the whole truth—including the parts you’re leaving out.

So the discipline becomes:

Tell it beautifully, but don’t lie.
Compress it, but don’t counterfeit it.
Choose the detail that reveals the whole.

And there’s an even stranger consequence: if God already knows the ending, then suspense cannot be the deepest purpose of a story.

The point becomes the telling itself—the particular path a consciousness takes through something that was already known.

That may be why the best stories can survive being spoiled.

You aren’t really asking, “What happens?”

You’re asking:

“What did it mean to be there?”

Audience of One.